INSERT INTO sections (id, recipe_id, number, title, description)
VALUES
  -- Recipe 1: Shakshuka (PUBLIC)
  (1, 1, 1, 'Caramelize Onions', 'This is the foundation of authentic French onion soup and requires patience. The slow caramelization process transforms ordinary yellow onions into deeply sweet, mahogany-colored ribbons that provide the rich, complex flavor base. You''ll want to slice your onions thinly and uniformly to ensure even cooking. The key is maintaining a medium-low temperature throughout - if the heat is too high, the onions will burn rather than caramelize. Stir occasionally, allowing the onions to develop color between stirs. This process typically takes 45-60 minutes, but the result is worth every minute. The natural sugars in the onions will concentrate and caramelize, creating that signature sweet-savory flavor that makes this soup legendary.'),
  (2, 1, 2, 'Build the Soup', 'Once your onions have reached that perfect deep golden-brown color, it''s time to transform them into soup. Deglaze the pan with wine to capture all those flavorful brown bits stuck to the bottom - this is pure flavor gold. Then add your beef broth, which should be high-quality and preferably homemade if possible. Fresh thyme sprigs and bay leaves add aromatic depth, while the salt and pepper should be adjusted carefully to taste. Allow the soup to simmer gently for at least 30 minutes, giving all the flavors time to marry together. The broth will reduce slightly and intensify in flavor. Some traditional recipes even simmer for up to an hour for maximum depth.'),
  (3, 1, 3, 'Gratinée and Serve', 'The gratinée topping is what elevates French onion soup from good to extraordinary. Start with a quality baguette sliced into rounds about 1 inch thick and toast them until completely dry and golden - this prevents them from getting soggy in the soup. Ladle the piping hot soup into individual oven-safe crocks, leaving about an inch from the top. Float the toasted bread on the surface and cover generously with freshly grated Gruyère cheese, making sure the cheese extends to the edges of the crock. Place under a preheated broiler until the cheese is melted, bubbling vigorously, and develops those beautiful golden-brown spots. The cheese should form a stretchy, savory blanket over the soup. Serve immediately while the cheese is still molten and the soup is steaming hot.'),

  -- Recipe 2: Grandma's Secret Meatballs (PRIVATE)
  (4, 2, 1, 'Make Dressing', 'The key to an authentic Caesar dressing is using quality ingredients and the proper emulsification technique. Start with room temperature egg yolks, which will help create a silky, stable emulsion. The anchovies are non-negotiable - they provide that signature umami depth that makes Caesar dressing so addictive. Mince them into a paste along with fresh garlic cloves. The garlic should be finely minced or even grated on a microplane for the best distribution of flavor. Fresh lemon juice adds brightness and acidity to cut through the richness. As you slowly drizzle in the olive oil while whisking constantly, you''ll watch the mixture transform from thin and separated to thick and creamy. This emulsification is crucial - adding the oil too quickly will cause the dressing to break. The final result should be thick enough to coat the back of a spoon and have a pale, creamy color.'),
  (5, 2, 2, 'Prepare Croutons', 'Homemade croutons are infinitely superior to store-bought and surprisingly easy to make. The best croutons start with slightly stale bread - day-old crusty bread is ideal because it absorbs the oil and seasonings without getting soggy. Cut the bread into uniform cubes, about 1 inch each, so they cook evenly. Toss them generously with good quality olive oil - don''t be shy here, as the oil carries flavor and helps achieve that golden, crispy exterior. Freshly minced garlic adds aromatic punch, while salt and freshly ground black pepper season throughout. Spread the seasoned bread cubes in a single layer on a baking sheet to ensure even toasting. Bake at 375°F, stirring halfway through, until they''re golden brown and completely crispy throughout, about 12-15 minutes. They should be crunchy all the way through, not just on the outside. Let them cool completely - they''ll crisp up even more as they cool.'),
  (6, 2, 3, 'Assemble Salad', 'Assembly is the final crucial step that brings all components together. Start with completely dry romaine lettuce - any water will dilute your carefully crafted dressing and make the salad soggy. The outer leaves can be torn into bite-sized pieces, while the tender inner leaves can be left larger for texture variation. Add about two-thirds of your dressing initially and toss vigorously, using your hands or tongs to ensure every leaf is coated. The leaves should glisten with dressing but not be drowning in it - you can always add more, but you can''t remove excess. Taste a leaf and adjust seasoning if needed. Add most of your croutons and toss again, reserving some for garnish. Finally, using a vegetable peeler, shave generous curls of Parmigiano-Reggiano directly over the salad. The fresh-shaved cheese adds both flavor and visual appeal. Top with the reserved croutons, an extra shower of Parmesan, and freshly cracked black pepper. Serve immediately while the croutons are still crispy.'),

  -- Recipe 3: Spaghetti Carbonara (PUBLIC)
  (7, 3, 1, 'Prepare Beef and Duxelles', 'Beef Wellington is an impressive centerpiece that requires careful preparation of its key components. Start with a high-quality beef tenderloin, trimmed of any silver skin and tied if necessary to maintain an even shape. Season it generously with salt and freshly ground black pepper. Heat a heavy skillet until it''s smoking hot - this high heat is essential for developing a proper crust. Sear the beef quickly on all sides, about 1-2 minutes per side, until it develops a deep golden-brown crust. This not only adds flavor but also helps seal in the juices. Remove immediately and let it cool completely. Meanwhile, prepare the duxelles, a finely minced mushroom mixture that''s been called the soul of Beef Wellington. Use a mix of mushrooms for complexity - cremini, shiitake, and porcini work beautifully. Mince them very finely, almost to a paste, and cook slowly with shallots in butter until all the moisture has evaporated. This concentration is crucial - any residual moisture will make your pastry soggy. Season with fresh thyme, salt, and pepper, then cool completely before assembly.'),
  (8, 3, 2, 'Wrap in Pastry', 'The wrapping technique is what transforms simple components into the legendary Beef Wellington. Start by laying out overlapping slices of thinly sliced prosciutto on a large sheet of plastic wrap, creating a rectangle slightly larger than your beef. The prosciutto serves multiple purposes - it adds flavor, helps hold the duxelles in place, and creates a moisture barrier to protect the pastry. Spread your cooled duxelles in an even layer over the prosciutto, leaving a small border. Place the cooled, seared beef in the center and use the plastic wrap to tightly roll the prosciutto and duxelles around the beef, creating a tight cylinder. Twist the ends of the plastic wrap to secure and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes - this helps everything set and makes wrapping in pastry easier. Roll out your puff pastry into a rectangle large enough to fully encase the beef bundle. Unwrap the beef from the plastic and place it seam-side down in the center of the pastry. Brush the edges with egg wash, then carefully wrap the pastry around the beef, pressing the seams to seal. Trim any excess pastry and crimp the edges.'),
  (9, 3, 3, 'Bake to Perfection', 'The final baking stage is where precision matters most - you want a golden, crispy pastry while achieving the perfect internal temperature for the beef. After chilling your wrapped Wellington for 30 minutes, brush the entire surface with egg wash for that beautiful golden sheen. Using a sharp knife, score decorative patterns on top - traditional diagonal lines or a lattice pattern work well, but don''t cut too deep or you''ll pierce the pastry. Make a small hole in the top to allow steam to escape during baking. Bake in a preheated 425°F oven for 25-30 minutes, watching carefully. The pastry should turn a deep golden brown and sound hollow when tapped. Use an instant-read thermometer inserted into the center - aim for 120-125°F for medium-rare. Remember, the beef will continue to cook as it rests, so slightly underdone is better than overdone. Let the Wellington rest for 10-15 minutes before slicing - this allows the juices to redistribute and makes for cleaner slices. Cut with a sharp serrated knife into thick slices, revealing the beautiful layers of golden pastry, prosciutto, duxelles, and perfectly pink beef.'),

  -- Recipe 4: Mom's Chocolate Cake (PRIVATE)
  (10, 4, 1, 'Make Mascarpone Cream', 'The mascarpone cream is the soul of this dessert, and achieving the perfect texture requires understanding the delicate nature of this rich Italian cheese and how it interacts with the other ingredients. Begin by ensuring all your ingredients are at room temperature, which is absolutely crucial for creating a smooth, lump-free mixture. Cold mascarpone will not blend properly and will result in a grainy, separated cream that lacks the luxurious silkiness that defines great tiramisu. Separate your eggs with meticulous care, ensuring not even a speck of yolk gets into the whites, as any fat will prevent them from whipping properly. Beat the egg yolks with granulated sugar using an electric mixer until the mixture becomes pale yellow, thick, and ribbony - when you lift the beaters, the mixture should fall back in thick ribbons that hold their shape on the surface for several seconds before slowly dissolving back into the mass. This process incorporates air and dissolves the sugar completely, creating a stable base for the mascarpone. Gently fold in the mascarpone cheese using a large rubber spatula, working in a figure-eight pattern and rotating the bowl as you fold. The goal is to incorporate the mascarpone completely while maintaining as much air as possible in the mixture. In a separate bowl, whip heavy cream to soft peaks and fold it into the mascarpone mixture for added lightness and stability. The finished cream should be thick enough to hold its shape but still spreadable, with a color somewhere between ivory and pale yellow, and a flavor that balances the rich, slightly tangy mascarpone with the sweetness of the sugar.'),
  (11, 4, 2, 'Soak Ladyfingers', 'The soaking of the ladyfingers is perhaps the most critical step in achieving perfect tiramisu texture - too brief and they remain dry and crunchy, too long and they become soggy and fall apart, losing their essential structure. Brew strong espresso using freshly ground coffee beans - the quality and strength of your coffee directly impacts the final flavor, so don''t skimp here. Traditional recipes call for extremely strong espresso, almost to the point of being bitter, as this coffee flavor will be mellowed by the sweet mascarpone cream and the cocoa powder. Add a few tablespoons of coffee liqueur like Kalamata rum, Marsala wine, or amaretto to the cooled espresso, which adds complexity and a subtle boozy undertone that elevates the dessert from simple to sophisticated. Have your ladyfinger cookies ready - these Italian sponge biscuits, called savoiardi, are specifically designed for tiramisu with their dry, porous texture that absorbs liquid without immediately falling apart. Working quickly and systematically, dip each ladyfinger into the coffee mixture for exactly 2-3 seconds per side - you want them saturated but not falling apart. The key is to move with purpose: dip, flip, dip, and immediately place in your dish. Some cooks prefer to brush the coffee onto the cookies for more control, but the traditional method is a quick dunk. As you work, you''ll develop a rhythm and an instinct for the right amount of soaking.'),
  (12, 4, 3, 'Layer and Chill', 'The final assembly and chilling process is where all your careful preparation comes together to create those beautiful, distinct layers that define a properly made tiramisu. Start with a rectangular or square dish that''s at least 9x13 inches and about 3 inches deep - you need depth for proper layering. Arrange your coffee-soaked ladyfingers in a single, tight layer across the bottom of the dish, fitting them like puzzle pieces and breaking some to fill gaps if necessary. You want complete coverage with no gaps where cream could seep through. Spread half of your mascarpone cream over this layer using an offset spatula, working gently to avoid disturbing the coffee-soaked cookies underneath. Create an even layer that reaches all the way to the edges and corners. Add a second layer of soaked ladyfingers on top of the cream, again working to achieve complete coverage. Spread the remaining mascarpone cream over this final cookie layer, smoothing the top with your spatula into an even, level surface. Using a fine-mesh sieve, dust the entire top generously with unsweetened cocoa powder - this provides not just visual appeal but also adds a slightly bitter chocolate note that balances the rich sweetness below. Cover tightly with plastic wrap and refrigerate for at least 6 hours, though overnight is even better. During this time, the flavors will meld together beautifully, the coffee will fully permeate the cookies, and the cream will firm up to the perfect sliceable consistency. When ready to serve, cut into squares with a sharp knife, wiping the blade clean between cuts for neat presentation. Each slice should reveal distinct layers of cream and coffee-soaked cookie, creating a stunning cross-section.'),

  -- Recipe 5: Margherita Pizza (PUBLIC)
  (13, 5, 1, 'Make Tomato Base', 'Building the tomato base for shakshuka is an exercise in patience and layering flavors, where each ingredient is added at precisely the right moment to create maximum impact. Start by heating a generous amount of high-quality olive oil in a large, deep skillet or shallow braising pan - cast iron works beautifully here as it retains heat evenly. Dice your onions and bell peppers into medium pieces, not too fine as you want them to maintain some texture and presence in the final dish. Add the onions first, cooking them over medium heat until they begin to soften and turn translucent, approximately 5-7 minutes. The key is to not rush this step - properly softened onions will dissolve slightly into the sauce, adding natural sweetness and body. Add your peppers and continue cooking until they too begin to soften, their edges just starting to develop some color. Now comes the spice blooming - add cumin, paprika, a touch of cayenne for heat, and any other aromatics like minced garlic. Stir constantly for about 60 seconds, just until the spices become incredibly fragrant but before they start to burn. This toasting process releases the essential oils in the spices and eliminates any raw, dusty flavor. Pour in crushed tomatoes - preferably San Marzano or another high-quality variety - and stir everything together. Add a pinch of sugar to balance the acidity of the tomatoes, salt to taste, and let the mixture simmer gently for 15-20 minutes. During this time, the sauce will thicken slightly, the flavors will meld together, and the oil will rise to the surface, creating those beautiful red puddles that are characteristic of properly made shakshuka. Taste and adjust seasonings - the sauce should be richly flavored, slightly sweet, with a gentle heat that builds gradually.'),
  (14, 5, 2, 'Poach Eggs', 'Poaching eggs directly in the tomato sauce is what transforms shakshuka from a simple tomato stew into a complete, satisfying meal, but it requires careful technique to achieve perfectly cooked eggs with runny yolks and just-set whites. Once your tomato base has simmered and thickened to the right consistency, reduce the heat to medium-low - you want the sauce to be gently bubbling but not at a rolling boil, as too much agitation will break up the eggs. Using the back of a large spoon, create six evenly spaced indentations or "wells" in the sauce, pressing down to create depressions that will hold the eggs. These wells should be fairly deep and defined, reaching almost to the bottom of the pan. Working carefully, crack each egg one at a time into a small bowl first - this allows you to remove any shell fragments and ensures the yolk is intact before adding it to the sauce. Gently slide each egg from the bowl into one of the prepared wells, taking care not to break the yolk. The whites will immediately start to set upon contact with the hot sauce. Once all eggs are in the pan, you have a choice: you can cover the pan with a lid to trap steam and cook the eggs from above, which is faster but can overcook the yolks, or you can leave it uncovered and baste the eggs occasionally with the hot sauce, which takes longer but gives you more control. Most traditional recipes call for covering the pan for 8-10 minutes, checking occasionally, until the whites are completely set but the yolks remain gloriously runny. The eggs should jiggle slightly when you shake the pan, indicating that liquid yolk remains inside.'),
  (15, 5, 3, 'Finish and Serve', 'The final touches and presentation of shakshuka are crucial - this is a dish meant to be enjoyed communally, with everyone gathering around the pan and scooping up the rich sauce and perfectly poached eggs with pieces of bread, so the visual appeal is important. Once your eggs are cooked to perfection - whites set, yolks still liquid - remove the pan from the heat immediately to prevent carry-over cooking. The residual heat in the pan can quickly overcook those carefully poached eggs if you''re not careful. Now for the garnishes that add freshness, color, and additional layers of flavor: scatter chopped fresh cilantro and parsley liberally over the entire surface - don''t be shy here, as the fresh herbs provide a bright, herbal contrast to the rich, spiced tomato sauce. Crumble a generous amount of feta cheese over everything - the salty, tangy cheese will melt slightly from the heat of the sauce and adds a creamy element that complements the eggs beautifully. Some versions include a final drizzle of excellent extra-virgin olive oil and perhaps a sprinkle of za''atar or sumac for an extra Middle Eastern touch. Serve the shakshuka directly from the pan - there''s something wonderfully rustic and inviting about presenting it this way. Have plenty of warm, crusty bread on hand for scooping - pita bread is traditional, but any good crusty bread will work. The goal is to break into those runny yolks, mixing them with the spiced tomato sauce and herbs, and soaking everything up with bread. This is messy, hands-on eating at its finest, perfect for a leisurely brunch with friends or family gathered around the table.'),

  -- Recipe 6: Nonna's Pasta Sauce (PRIVATE)
  (16, 6, 1, 'Mix Meatball Ingredients', 'Creating the perfect meatball mixture is an art form that Nonna has perfected over decades, combining not just ingredients but technique and intuition to achieve meatballs that are tender, flavorful, and hold together beautifully without becoming dense or tough. The foundation begins with the meat blend - traditionally a combination of ground beef, pork, and veal, each contributing its own characteristics to the final product. The beef provides rich, meaty flavor and helps the meatballs hold their shape; the pork adds moisture and a subtle sweetness; the veal brings tenderness and a delicate texture that keeps the meatballs from becoming heavy. The ratio matters, though Nonna claims she does it by feel rather than measurement. To this meat blend, add fresh breadcrumbs that have been soaked in milk - this panade is crucial for keeping the meatballs tender and moist rather than dense. Squeeze the excess milk from the breadcrumbs before adding them to the meat. Add eggs for binding - they help hold everything together while also contributing to the tender texture. The secret spice blend comes next, and this is where family members get frustrated because Nonna''s measurements vary each time she makes them. The blend includes the expected garlic, parsley, salt, and pepper, but there are other elements - hints of fennel seed, perhaps some red pepper flakes, definitely fresh basil, and something else that nobody has quite identified. Mix everything together using your hands, working gently but thoroughly to distribute all ingredients evenly throughout the meat. The temperature of your hands actually matters - cold hands help keep the fat in the meat from melting, which would lead to tough, dry meatballs.'),
  (17, 6, 2, 'Shape and Simmer', 'The shaping and cooking process requires a delicate touch and an understanding of how to develop flavor through proper browning while keeping the interior moist and tender. Once your mixture is properly combined, resist the urge to work it too much - overhandling will result in tough, dense meatballs. With wet hands (keep a bowl of water nearby), scoop about 2 tablespoons of the mixture and roll it gently between your palms into a sphere. The meatballs should be uniform in size for even cooking, roughly the size of a golf ball or slightly larger. Set them on a parchment-lined baking sheet as you work. Some cooks prefer to refrigerate the formed meatballs for 30 minutes before cooking, which helps them hold together better, though Nonna rarely bothers with this step. Heat oil in a large, deep skillet and carefully add the meatballs in batches - don''t crowd the pan or they''ll steam rather than brown. Cook them just until they develop a rich, brown crust on all sides, turning them carefully with tongs. They don''t need to be cooked through at this point as they''ll finish cooking in the sauce. The browning is crucial for developing deep, savory flavor through the Maillard reaction. Once all meatballs are browned, transfer them to your simmering tomato sauce, spooning sauce over them to coat completely. Cover and let them simmer gently for at least 1.5 to 2 hours - yes, hours. This slow, gentle cooking allows the meatballs to become incredibly tender, almost falling apart at the touch of a fork, while simultaneously enriching the sauce with their flavor. The meatballs will absorb some of the sauce while also releasing their own juices back into it, creating a beautiful symbiosis.'),

  -- Recipe 7: Thai Green Curry (PUBLIC)
  (18, 7, 1, 'Prep Ingredients', 'Proper preparation and organization - what professional chefs call mise en place - is absolutely critical for executing carbonara successfully, as the final cooking happens very quickly and there''s no time to search for ingredients or measure anything once you begin. Start with your pancetta or guanciale - if you''re lucky enough to find guanciale, which is cured pork jowl, it''s worth the extra effort as it has a richer, more complex flavor than pancetta. Cut it into small cubes or thin strips, about 1/4 inch thick. The pieces should be small enough to crisp up nicely but large enough to provide textural contrast in the finished dish. Set these aside within easy reach of your stove. Crack your eggs into a bowl - you''ll need about 4 whole eggs for a pound of pasta, though some recipes use a combination of whole eggs and yolks for extra richness. Beat them lightly with a fork until the yolks and whites are just combined. Add a generous amount of freshly grated Pecorino Romano cheese to the eggs - about a cup or more. Use real Pecorino Romano, not the pre-grated stuff in the green can, as the difference in flavor is night and day. Pecorino is made from sheep''s milk and has a sharp, salty, slightly funky flavor that''s essential to authentic carbonara. Grind in a generous amount of black pepper - more than you think you need - as this provides the "carbo" part of carbonara and should be a prominent flavor. Mix everything together into a thick, paste-like consistency. Fill your largest pot with water, salt it generously - it should taste like the sea - and bring it to a rolling boil. The timing of everything matters here: you want your pasta cooking, your pancetta crisping, and your egg mixture at room temperature all happening simultaneously.'),
  (19, 7, 2, 'Cook Pasta and Pancetta', 'The timing and coordination between cooking the pasta and rendering the pancetta is the make-or-break moment for carbonara - both elements need to finish at precisely the right moment and be combined while everything is at exactly the right temperature. Add your spaghetti to the boiling water and cook it until just al dente - it should still have a slight firmness when you bite into it, as it will continue cooking slightly when combined with the hot pancetta fat and egg mixture. While the pasta cooks, begin working on your pancetta. Place a large skillet over medium heat - not too hot, as you want to render out the fat slowly rather than burning the meat. Add your diced pancetta to the cold pan and let it gradually heat up. This slow rendering allows maximum fat to melt out while the meat pieces crisp up beautifully. No additional oil is needed - the pancetta has more than enough fat of its own. Cook, stirring occasionally, until the pancetta is golden brown and crispy, with the fat fully rendered and collecting in the bottom of the pan. This usually takes about 8-10 minutes, roughly the time it takes for your pasta to cook. The timing is crucial: you want the pasta to finish cooking just as the pancetta reaches peak crispiness. About 30 seconds before your pasta is done, use a ladle or measuring cup to scoop out at least a cup of the starchy pasta cooking water - this liquid gold will help create the creamy sauce. Drain the pasta but don''t rinse it - you want to keep that starchy coating that will help the sauce cling to each strand.'),
  (20, 7, 3, 'Combine and Toss', 'This is the moment where everything comes together, and it requires swift action and precise temperature control to achieve that perfectly creamy, clingy sauce without scrambling the eggs into clumps. As soon as you drain the pasta, remove your pan of pancetta from the heat and let it cool for about 30 seconds - this is crucial, as if the pan is too hot, the eggs will scramble when you add them. The pan should still be very warm but not actively cooking. Add the hot, drained pasta directly to the pan with the pancetta and its rendered fat. Toss everything together vigorously, coating every strand of pasta with the pancetta fat. Now, working off heat but with the pan still warm, pour in your egg and cheese mixture. Immediately start tossing the pasta with tongs or two forks, working quickly and thoroughly to coat every strand. The residual heat from the pasta and the warm pan will gently cook the eggs, transforming them from raw to creamy without scrambling them. If the sauce seems too thick and clumpy, add splashes of the reserved pasta water, a little at a time, tossing constantly. The pasta water''s starch will help emulsify everything into a silky, creamy sauce that clings to each strand of spaghetti. Keep tossing and adding pasta water until you achieve the desired consistency - the sauce should be creamy and flowing, coating the pasta in a glossy sheen rather than sitting in clumps. Taste and adjust seasoning with more cheese or pepper if needed. Serve immediately in warm bowls, topped with additional grated Pecorino and a generous grinding of black pepper. The pasta should be steaming hot, with a sauce that''s simultaneously creamy and light, allowing the flavors of the eggs, cheese, pork, and pepper to shine through clearly. This is carbonara in its truest, most elemental form - proof that with just a handful of quality ingredients and proper technique, you can create something transcendent.'),

  -- Recipe 8: Uncle's BBQ Sauce (PRIVATE)
  (21, 8, 1, 'Marinate Chicken', 'The marination process is where the magic begins for this tandoori-style chicken, transforming plain chicken pieces into flavor-packed, tender morsels that are aromatic, spiced, and incredibly delicious. Begin by cutting your chicken into uniform pieces - if using breast meat, cut it into 1 to 1.5 inch cubes for maximum surface area to absorb the marinade and for even cooking. Thighs can be left larger or bone-in if you prefer, though they''ll take longer to cook. Pat the chicken completely dry with paper towels - this is important because the marinade will adhere better to dry meat than wet. In a large bowl, combine thick, full-fat yogurt with your spice blend: freshly grated ginger, minced garlic, garam masala, ground cumin, paprika for color, cayenne for heat, and perhaps ground coriander and turmeric. The yogurt serves multiple purposes - its acidity tenderizes the meat, its fat carries the fat-soluble flavors into the chicken, and its proteins help create that characteristic slightly charred exterior when grilled. Add a squeeze of fresh lemon juice and salt to taste. Mix everything into a thick, vibrant orange-red paste, adjusting spices to taste - some prefer more heat, others more aromatic warmth. Add your chicken pieces and use your hands to massage the marinade into every surface, ensuring complete coverage. Transfer everything to a container or zip-top bag, press out excess air, and refrigerate for at least 4 hours, though overnight is ideal. During this time, the acids and spices will penetrate the meat, flavoring it throughout while the yogurt enzymes tenderize the proteins.'),
  (22, 8, 2, 'Grill Chicken', 'Grilling the marinated chicken requires high heat and careful attention to achieve that perfect combination of charred exterior and juicy, tender interior that characterizes the best tandoori chicken. The traditional method uses a tandoor oven, reaching temperatures of 900°F or more, but a standard grill or broiler can produce excellent results with the right technique. If using a grill, preheat it to high heat and oil the grates well to prevent sticking. If using a broiler, position the rack in the upper third of the oven and preheat the broiler to high. Thread your marinated chicken pieces onto metal skewers, spacing them slightly apart for even air circulation and heat penetration. If using wooden skewers, soak them in water for at least 30 minutes to prevent burning. Shake off excess marinade from the chicken pieces - you want a coating but not so much that it drips and causes flare-ups. Place the skewers on the grill or under the broiler and cook, turning every 3-4 minutes to ensure even cooking and charring on all sides. The high heat will cause the sugars in the yogurt and the surface of the chicken to caramelize and char in spots, creating those irresistible blackened edges that are packed with concentrated flavor. The chicken is done when it reaches an internal temperature of 165°F and the juices run clear when pierced - usually 12-15 minutes total, depending on the size of your pieces and the heat of your grill. The exterior should be deeply colored with areas of char, while the interior remains juicy and tender.'),
  (23, 8, 3, 'Make Curry Sauce', 'The curry sauce brings everything together, providing a rich, creamy vehicle that complements and enhances the grilled chicken while adding another layer of complex spice flavor. In a large, heavy-bottomed pan, melt several tablespoons of butter or ghee over medium heat. Add diced onions and cook until they''re soft and beginning to turn golden brown - about 10 minutes. Don''t rush this step, as the caramelization of the onions adds sweetness and depth to the final sauce. Add freshly grated ginger and minced garlic, cooking until fragrant, about 1-2 minutes. Stir constantly to prevent burning. Add your ground spices - garam masala, cumin, coriander, turmeric, and a touch of cayenne for heat. Toast these spices in the butter for about 30 seconds until they release their essential oils and become incredibly aromatic. Pour in crushed tomatoes and stir everything together, scraping up any browned bits from the bottom of the pan. Let this mixture simmer for 10-15 minutes until it thickens and the raw tomato flavor mellows. Now add heavy cream - the amount depends on how rich and creamy you want your sauce. Stir it in gradually, watching the sauce transform into a beautiful orange-pink color. Season with salt and add a touch of sugar if the sauce seems too acidic. Add your grilled chicken pieces to the sauce, spooning the sauce over them to coat completely. Simmer everything together for 10-15 minutes, allowing the chicken to absorb some of the sauce while the flavors meld together. Finish with fresh chopped cilantro, a drizzle of cream for presentation, and perhaps a squeeze of lime juice for brightness. The sauce should be thick enough to coat the back of a spoon, deeply flavored, and creamy without being heavy.'),

  -- Recipe 9: Quinoa Salad (PUBLIC)
  (24, 9, 1, 'Mix Batter', 'Creating perfect pancake batter is about understanding the chemistry of leavening and the importance of not over-developing the gluten, which would result in tough, rubbery pancakes instead of the light, fluffy clouds we''re aiming for. Start with two separate bowls - one for dry ingredients and one for wet. In your dry bowl, whisk together all-purpose flour, granulated sugar, baking powder, and a pinch of salt. The baking powder is your leavening agent, creating those bubbles that make pancakes rise and become fluffy. Make sure your baking powder is fresh - if it''s old, it won''t have the same lifting power. In your wet bowl, whisk together buttermilk, eggs, and melted butter that''s been cooled slightly so it doesn''t scramble the eggs. Buttermilk is key here - its acidity reacts with the baking powder to create extra lift, and it adds a subtle tang that makes pancakes more interesting than plain milk would. If you don''t have buttermilk, you can make a substitute by adding a tablespoon of lemon juice or vinegar to regular milk and letting it sit for 5 minutes. Make a well in the center of your dry ingredients and pour in the wet ingredients all at once. Using a whisk or large spoon, gently fold the wet into the dry, mixing just until the ingredients are barely combined. This is crucial: you should still see lumps and streaks of flour in the batter. Overmixing develops gluten, which makes pancakes tough and prevents them from rising properly. Those lumps will work themselves out during cooking. Let the batter rest for 5-10 minutes while your griddle heats - this allows the flour to fully hydrate and the leavening to activate, resulting in lighter, fluffier pancakes.'),
  (25, 9, 2, 'Cook Pancakes', 'The cooking process is where technique and timing come together to create those perfectly golden, fluffy pancakes with crispy edges that everyone dreams about but often struggles to achieve at home. Preheat your griddle or large skillet to 375°F if you have a temperature-controlled surface, or medium heat if you don''t. Test the temperature by flicking a few drops of water onto the surface - they should dance and evaporate quickly but not instantly, indicating the perfect temperature. Lightly grease the cooking surface with butter or neutral oil - don''t use too much or your pancakes will fry rather than properly cook through. For each pancake, pour about 1/4 cup of batter onto the hot griddle, using the bottom of your measuring cup or a ladle to spread it slightly into a round shape if needed. The batter should sizzle gently when it hits the surface but not spatter violently. Immediately after pouring, while the surface is still wet, scatter fresh blueberries onto each pancake - they''ll sink slightly into the batter and cook along with it, creating pockets of jammy fruit throughout. Resist the temptation to press down on the pancakes or move them around - just let them cook undisturbed. Watch for bubbles to form on the surface - they''ll start appearing around the edges and gradually work their way toward the center. When the bubbles have mostly popped and the edges look dry and slightly set, and the underside is deep golden brown (check by gently lifting a corner with your spatula), it''s time to flip. This usually takes about 2-3 minutes. Flip the pancakes confidently and quickly - hesitation leads to messy flips. Cook for another 2 minutes on the second side until golden brown. The second side will never be quite as evenly colored as the first, which is completely normal.'),
  (26, 9, 3, 'Serve Hot', 'Proper serving and presentation transforms pancakes from breakfast into an experience, with careful attention to temperature, accompaniments, and the art of building an appealing stack that photographs beautifully and tastes even better. As each batch comes off the griddle, transfer the pancakes to a warm oven (200°F) to keep them hot and fluffy while you finish cooking the rest. Stack them on an oven-safe plate, placing a clean kitchen towel between each batch to absorb any excess steam that might make them soggy. When all pancakes are cooked, it''s time to build your stacks. For each serving, place 3-4 pancakes on a warm plate, arranging them slightly offset from each other so you can see the edges of each pancake - this looks more appealing than a perfectly aligned stack. Place a generous pat of cold butter on top of the stack - it should be cold enough to hold its shape initially but will gradually melt into the hot pancakes, creating those irresistible little golden puddles. The contrast between cold butter and hot pancakes is wonderful. Warm your maple syrup slightly before serving - warm syrup soaks into the pancakes more readily and doesn''t cool them down. Pour it generously over the stack, letting it cascade down the sides and pool around the bottom. Scatter additional fresh blueberries around the plate and perhaps a light dusting of powdered sugar for visual appeal. Some people like to add a dollop of whipped cream or a spoonful of Greek yogurt for extra richness. Serve immediately while everything is hot - pancakes are best eaten fresh and piping hot, when the exteriors are still slightly crispy and the interiors are at their fluffiest.'),

  -- Recipe 10: Dad's Chili Recipe (PRIVATE)
  (27, 10, 1, 'Prepare Eggplant', 'The preparation of eggplant for moussaka is crucial for achieving the proper texture and preventing the dish from becoming watery or bitter, requiring specific techniques that might seem tedious but make all the difference in the final result. Select large, firm eggplants that feel heavy for their size with taut, glossy skin - these will have fewer seeds and better texture. Using a sharp knife, slice the eggplants lengthwise into rounds approximately 1/4 to 3/8 inch thick. Consistency is important here - if slices vary too much in thickness, they''ll cook unevenly, with some burning while others remain undercooked. Arrange the slices in a single layer on baking sheets or directly on your work surface lined with paper towels. Generously sprinkle both sides with salt - this draws out the bitter juices and excess moisture that would otherwise make your moussaka soggy. You''ll see droplets of liquid forming on the surface within minutes. Let the salted eggplant sit for at least 30 minutes, up to an hour. After the resting period, rinse the slices under cold water to remove the salt and pat them completely dry with paper towels or clean kitchen towels - this step is important to remove any residual bitterness along with the salt. The eggplant slices should now feel firmer and less spongy than they were before salting. Now you have a choice for cooking: you can brush them lightly with olive oil and grill them until they develop nice char marks and become tender, or you can roast them in a hot oven (around 425°F) until golden brown on both sides, or you can even fry them in olive oil until golden. Each method produces a slightly different result, but all three work well. The key is to cook them until they''re fully tender but still hold their shape - if they fall apart, you''ve gone too far.'),
  (28, 10, 2, 'Make Meat Sauce', 'The meat sauce is the heart of moussaka, and unlike quick ragus, this requires time and attention to develop the deep, complex flavors that make the dish memorable, with layers of spice and richness that come from both the choice of meat and the cooking technique. Traditional moussaka uses ground lamb for its distinctive flavor, though ground beef or a mixture of the two is common and produces excellent results. Heat olive oil in a large, heavy-bottomed pot over medium-high heat. Add your ground meat and break it up with a wooden spoon, cooking without stirring initially to allow it to develop a good brown crust on the bottom. Once you see significant browning, break up the meat more thoroughly and continue cooking until all the pieces are deeply browned and any liquid has evaporated. This browning is crucial - it develops through the Maillard reaction and creates layers of savory flavor that form the foundation of your sauce. Remove the meat and set aside, then add diced onions to the same pot, scraping up all those flavorful browned bits from the bottom. Cook until the onions are soft and golden, then add minced garlic and cook for another minute. Return the meat to the pot and add tomato paste, cooking it for a minute or two until it darkens slightly and loses its raw smell. Add crushed tomatoes, red wine for depth, and your spice blend - cinnamon is essential for authentic moussaka, providing warmth rather than obvious cinnamon flavor, along with oregano, bay leaves, and perhaps a touch of allspice or nutmeg. Season with salt and pepper and add a pinch of sugar to balance the acidity of the tomatoes. Bring everything to a simmer, then reduce heat to low and let it cook gently for at least 30 minutes, stirring occasionally. The sauce should thicken considerably and become rich and complex in flavor. Taste and adjust seasoning - the sauce should be well-seasoned on its own as it needs to flavor the other bland components of the dish.'),
  (29, 10, 3, 'Layer and Bake', 'The final assembly and baking of moussaka is where all your careful preparation pays off, creating that stunning cross-section of distinct layers that makes this dish so visually impressive and delicious, requiring both organization and precision in the layering process. Have all your components ready: cooked eggplant slices, the rich meat sauce, and a freshly made béchamel sauce (thick, creamy white sauce made from butter, flour, and milk, often enriched with egg yolks and cheese for moussaka). Select a deep baking dish - a 9x13 inch pan works well for a standard recipe. Lightly oil the bottom of the dish to prevent sticking. Begin layering: arrange half of your cooked eggplant slices in the bottom of the dish in a single, overlapping layer, covering the entire surface. They can overlap slightly but should create a cohesive layer with no gaps. Spread all of the meat sauce over the eggplant layer, distributing it evenly from edge to edge and pressing down gently to eliminate air pockets. Top the meat sauce with the remaining eggplant slices, again arranging them in a single, even layer. Finally, pour your béchamel sauce over the top, using an offset spatula to spread it evenly all the way to the edges and into the corners - you want complete coverage with no meat sauce peeking through. For extra richness and color, sprinkle the top with grated cheese, typically a mixture of Parmesan and kefalotyri or other Greek cheese. Bake in a preheated 350°F oven for 45-60 minutes until the top is golden brown and bubbling around the edges. The béchamel should develop a beautiful golden crust with darker brown spots. Remove from the oven and let it rest for at least 20-30 minutes before cutting - this resting period is crucial as it allows the layers to set and makes for much cleaner slicing. Cut into squares and serve warm, admiring that perfect cross-section of layers that represents hours of careful preparation.'),

  -- Recipe 11: Lasagna Bolognese (PUBLIC)
  (30, 11, 1, 'Make Shortbread Crust', 'The buttery, crumbly shortbread crust serves as the perfect foundation for tangy lemon bars, providing textural contrast and a neutral, slightly sweet base that allows the bright lemon flavor to shine, but it requires proper technique to achieve that perfect sandy, melt-in-your-mouth texture. Start with cold butter cut into small cubes - using cold butter is crucial as it creates that characteristic short, crumbly texture. If your kitchen is very warm, you might even chill your mixing bowl. In a food processor or large bowl, combine all-purpose flour with powdered sugar rather than granulated - the fine texture of powdered sugar integrates more smoothly and creates a more tender crust. Add a pinch of salt to enhance all the flavors. Add your cold butter cubes and process in short pulses (if using a processor) or cut in with a pastry blender or two forks until the mixture resembles coarse crumbs or wet sand. The pieces of butter should be about the size of peas - if you work it more than this, the crust will be tough rather than tender. You want the mixture to just barely hold together when pressed. Press this mixture firmly and evenly into the bottom of a 9x13 inch pan, using the bottom of a measuring cup or your fingers to create an even layer with uniform thickness, paying special attention to the corners where the crust tends to be thinner. The crust should go all the way to the edges of the pan. Prick the bottom all over with a fork to prevent bubbling during baking. Bake in a preheated 350°F oven for 18-22 minutes until the crust is set and lightly golden brown, especially around the edges. It should look dry rather than wet and raw. The crust will continue to firm up as it cools, but it should already feel set when you remove it from the oven.'),
  (31, 11, 2, 'Prepare Lemon Filling', 'The lemon filling is where the magic happens, transforming simple ingredients into that perfect balance of sweet and tart with a smooth, custardy texture that sets up beautifully when baked and chilled, creating those clean-cutting bars that everyone loves. The key to exceptional lemon bars is using fresh lemon juice - bottled juice simply cannot compare and will result in a flat, artificial-tasting filling. You''ll need about 4-5 large lemons to yield the 1/2 cup of juice required, though this varies with the juiciness of your fruit. Roll each lemon firmly on the counter before juicing to break down the membranes and release more juice. Zest two of the lemons before juicing them, using a microplane or fine grater to remove only the bright yellow outer layer while avoiding the bitter white pith underneath. The zest contains intensely flavored oils that add another dimension of lemon flavor. In a large bowl, whisk together eggs with granulated sugar until the mixture is light and slightly frothy - this usually takes 2-3 minutes of vigorous whisking. The sugar should be completely dissolved and the mixture should have increased slightly in volume. Add your fresh lemon juice and zest, whisking to combine. The acidity of the lemon will cause the mixture to thicken slightly. Sift in a small amount of all-purpose flour - just 1/4 cup for the whole recipe - whisking constantly to prevent lumps. The flour provides structure and helps the filling set up properly during baking while maintaining that creamy, custardy texture. The filling should be smooth, pale yellow, and quite liquid - don''t worry, it will set beautifully during baking. Pour this mixture over your hot, pre-baked crust immediately after removing the crust from the oven - pouring onto a hot crust helps prevent a soggy bottom and ensures the filling adheres properly to the base.'),
  (32, 11, 3, 'Bake and Cool', 'The final baking and cooling process is critical for achieving lemon bars with the perfect texture - neither too runny nor too firm, with a smooth, glossy top and clean-cutting texture that makes for beautiful presentation and easy serving. Return your pan with the filled crust to the 350°F oven and bake for 20-25 minutes, watching carefully. The filling is done when it no longer jiggles in the center when you gently shake the pan - there should be a slight wobble around the edges, but the center should be set. If you see significant waves or liquid movement, it needs more time. The top should be set and may have a slight sheen but shouldn''t brown - if it starts to brown, your oven is too hot or you''ve baked it too long. Remove from the oven and let the bars cool in the pan at room temperature for about an hour. During this time, the filling will continue to set due to carry-over cooking, and the dramatic temperature difference will fade. Once cooled to room temperature, refrigerate the entire pan for at least 2 hours, though overnight is even better. The chilling is essential - it allows the filling to firm up to the perfect sliceable consistency. When ready to serve, remove from the refrigerator and let sit at room temperature for about 10 minutes to take the chill off. Using a sharp knife dipped in hot water and wiped clean between each cut, slice the lemon bars into squares. The hot, clean knife ensures clean cuts with smooth edges. Just before serving, dust the tops generously with powdered sugar using a fine-mesh sieve - this not only looks beautiful but adds a final touch of sweetness that perfectly complements the tart lemon filling. The bars should cut cleanly, revealing distinct layers of golden shortbread and sunny yellow lemon filling, with no crumbling or squishing. Store any leftovers covered in the refrigerator for up to a week.'),

  -- Recipe 12: Family Curry Powder (PRIVATE)
  (33, 12, 1, 'Make Cake Layers', 'The chocolate cake layers are legendary in our family, and while Mom won''t reveal the complete recipe, we know that achieving the perfect texture - incredibly moist, tender, and rich without being heavy or dense - requires specific techniques and that mysterious secret ingredient that she adds at just the right moment. The process begins like most cakes: creaming room-temperature butter with sugar until light and fluffy, which incorporates air and creates the foundation for a tender crumb. Beat in eggs one at a time, making sure each is fully incorporated before adding the next. In a separate bowl, whisk together flour, high-quality Dutch-process cocoa powder, baking soda, baking powder, and salt. The combination of leavening agents creates the perfect rise and texture. In yet another container, combine buttermilk with strong coffee or espresso - coffee enhances chocolate flavor without making the cake taste like coffee. The acidity of the buttermilk tenderizes the crumb and reacts with the baking soda for extra lift. Here''s where Mom does something different - she alternates adding the dry and wet ingredients to the butter mixture, beginning and ending with dry, but at some point during this process, she adds the secret ingredient. Family members have speculated it might be mayonnaise, sour cream, or even a vegetable like shredded zucchini or beets, but no one can say for certain. Whatever it is, it creates an impossibly moist texture that stays fresh for days. Mix just until combined - overmixing develops gluten and creates a tough cake. The batter should be smooth, glossy, and fairly thin - don''t worry, it''ll bake up beautifully. Divide evenly between two greased and floured 9-inch round cake pans and bake at 350°F for 30-35 minutes until a toothpick inserted in the center comes out with just a few moist crumbs.'),
  (34, 12, 2, 'Frost and Assemble', 'The frosting is what truly elevates this cake from excellent to legendary - it''s intensely chocolatey yet somehow light and silky, with a texture that spreads like a dream and a flavor that keeps people coming back for more, and like the cake itself, it contains elements that Mom keeps to herself. Start with room-temperature butter - this is crucial for proper texture. Beat the butter until it''s light and fluffy, then gradually add powdered sugar, mixing well between additions. Add high-quality cocoa powder, a pinch of salt, vanilla extract, and enough heavy cream to reach the desired consistency. The frosting should be thick enough to hold its shape but spreadable without tearing the delicate cake layers. Here''s where Mom''s frosting gets interesting - there''s something else she adds that gives it an almost mousse-like texture and a subtle flavor complexity that nobody can quite place. Some think it might be melted chocolate for extra richness, others suspect cream cheese for tanginess, and still others swear she uses coffee or even a liqueur. Whatever the secret components, the result is frosting perfection. Once your cake layers have cooled completely - and I mean completely, as warm cakes will cause the frosting to melt - place one layer on your serving plate. Spread about a third of the frosting in an even layer over the top. Place the second layer on top, making sure it''s level. Use the remaining frosting to cover the top and sides of the cake, working with an offset spatula to create smooth, even coverage. Mom has a specific technique for getting that perfectly smooth finish that makes the cake look professionally done. Some people like to create decorative swirls or patterns in the frosting, while others prefer the sleek, smooth modern look. Either way, the finished cake should be spectacular - deeply chocolate in color, with frosting that gleams slightly and layers that are visible when you cut into it. Let the frosted cake sit at room temperature for an hour before serving to allow the frosting to set slightly, making for cleaner slicing.');

SELECT setval('sections_id_seq', (SELECT MAX(id) FROM sections));
